92186045417] [17592186180800 #edb/ref 1080 "y =-x + \\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180800 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180800 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b5474311-af45-5f07-a416-7463c4f0e588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180801 #edb/ref 1080 "y =-x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180801 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180801 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d286cda9-c8b0-5c6f-a8c8-27556bd4031a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180802 #edb/ref 1080 "y =-\\sqrt{2}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180802 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180802 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f22015d0-c5ec-5aac-a089-d4b5684b4c22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180803 #edb/ref 1080 "y = x - \\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180803 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180803 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f7a76679-88da-5d8d-ae4c-fdabc35770f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180804 #edb/ref 1080 "y = x + \\sqrt{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180804 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180804 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38599052-4878-5204-ba74-69b12624951d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184078 #edb/ref 1069 "e-629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184078 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1+2\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, given that\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{\\cos\\theta + 2\\cos(2\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta + 2\\sin(2\\theta)}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dbb258d-fe71-5e7b-9501-c88ffdb6fe86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184078 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P$, we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{\\cos\\theta + 2\\cos(2\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta + 2\\sin(2\\theta)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P$.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = π/2} \\\\\n&= - (\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\cos (π))/(\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\sin (π)) \\\\\n&= - (0 + 2(-1))/(1 + 2(0)) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\cos θ)\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 + 2 \\cdot 0) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\\\\ny_{1} &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\cos θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 + 2 \\cdot 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,1)$.\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 1 &= 2(x - 0) \\\\\ny &= 2x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30b1aedfd6187ed1f936ef1faf25fcb8b99857103c656163a3e6897e5c6c5038.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184120 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184120 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fc/fc3ec38b91c71be3e9638dfa0127f1baab5d11d30e6156d863b36758f6751e7d.png)\n\nFind the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r = 1 - 2\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$, given that\n$y'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta-2\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad x'(\\theta) = 2\\sin(2\\theta) - \\sin\\theta$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184120 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59156f24-72a1-5642-ac66-f84002c87d0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184120 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P$, we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta-2\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad x'(\\theta) = 2\\sin(2\\theta) - \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos θ - 2\\cos (2θ))/(2\\sin (2θ) - \\sin θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P$.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = 3π/2} \\\\\n&= ((\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) - 2\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2})))/((2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}) - \\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}))) \\\\\n&= ((\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) - 2\\cos (3π)))/((2\\sin (3π) - \\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}))) \\\\\n&= (0 - 2(-1))/(0 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 + 2}{0 + 1} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (1 - 2\\cos θ)\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (1 - 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}))\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 - 2 \\cdot 0) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\\\\ny_{1} &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (1 - 2\\cos θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (1 - 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}))\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 - 2 \\cdot 0) \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(0, -1 \\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal.\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= -\\frac{1}{m}(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= -\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (x - 0) \\\\\ny + 1 &= -\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Tangent Lines to Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184177 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2216cdfc-7f2a-5c53-b10b-2d66f35fbf32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Normal Lines to Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184215 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3e95ede7-306b-55e1-9245-7493f0afb514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1045 "Tangent and Normal Lines to Polar Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186185006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184419 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "af91633b-288c-52bd-a641-7172621608f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186180799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184437 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r = \\sin (2θ)$ at $θ = \\frac{3π}{4}$, given that\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = (3\\sin (3θ) - \\sin θ)/(3\\cos (3θ) + \\cos θ)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca4cdd59-0ef8-55a6-b3e4-91d9fcc40f65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184437 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1061 2206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0ead2f8-7726-5a22-ad4d-582c7b4b8968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184944 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1061 37487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6c2d34a-6683-50c4-a82e-b71e985d4ed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185001 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1061 37501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a1e51e4b-71cf-5749-aae1-c001b3a5a59b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/a0/a0b4ad67ea80f6d6eec3a15b3df18d551d84263c7424405588bd15162ef4e7c2.png)\n\nThe polar curve shown above is defined as\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = 4 - 3\\cos \\theta, \\qquad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. The tangent to the curve at $P$ is shown in the diagram.\n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at $P$, we follow four steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing techniques that we've previously discussed, we can show that\n\n$$\ny^{′}(θ) = 4\\cos θ - 3\\cos 2θ, x^{′}(θ) = 3\\sin 2θ - 4\\sin θ\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\cos θ - 3\\cos 2θ}{3\\sin 2θ - 4\\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P$.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = π/2} \\\\\n&= (4\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - 3\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(3\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}) - 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= (4\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - 3\\cos (π))/(3\\sin (π) - 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 + 3}{0 - 4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos θ)\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 0 \\\\\ny_{1} &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(0, 4 \\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 4 &= -\\frac{3}{4} \\cdot (x - 0) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{4}x + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1065 6186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "febc7b49-9662-5e78-a7a5-cbe9b1f596ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185342 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
